00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:34.940
And it seems ,net was open
sourced some time ago.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.210
This effort has now
been doable, which is,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.570
you can now run .net
applications on Google Club

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.330
platform.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.020
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
this is pretty cool.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.260
I know some people
would be like, but wait,

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.600
couldn't I have just spun
up a Windows instance before

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.200
and done this myself regardless?

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.670
But I think the cool thing
is we've got now .net client

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.030
libraries for connecting to
things like Cloud Datastore

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.800
and other services that we have.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.409
So you want to not just run
just regular old applications,

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:02.450
but you want to interact
with some of the managed

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.310
services we have, I think that's
a really, really cool offering.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.330
There's a blog post, and
we'll put a link to it

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:10.370
on the show notes.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:13.830
But basically, the important
thing that I think is very

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:18.090
exciting is the fact that if you
like writing .net applications

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.600
and you really
know how to do it,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:26.040
now you can still do it and use
some of the infrastructure that

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.480
Google Cloud Platform offers.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.240
So one of them is
Google Cloud Datastore,

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.510
we talked about many times.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.560
So basically, this
way of basically

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.170
storing data that scales
is completely managed,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.680
so you don't have
to care about it.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.350
There's a bunch of examples.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.170
There's a Hello World tutorial,
there's a bookshelf tutorial.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.762
But basically, give
it a try and let us

00:02:45.762 --> 00:02:46.970
know what you think about it.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.652
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that'd be great.

