00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.390
So thanks so much for
coming to talk to us today

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.257
about Pocket Gems, about War
Dragons, about the game itself.

00:03:26.257 --> 00:03:27.840
But why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.760
about who you are, what you do
all that sort of good stuff.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.110
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: So
I'm a software engineer,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.660
I've been a software engineer
for I don't know how long.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.680
I started when I was 15,
even before college I started

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.590
programming, and today I'm
42 so you do the math there

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.040
I don't think I've ever done
anything else in my life.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.010
I love it.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:52.360
And mostly I've worked in Brazil
on non-gaming related stuff.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.140
Some boring corporate stuff.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.770
And then at some
point, I started

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.410
coming here to San
Francisco a lot,

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.940
And I just wanted to move here.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.640
I was like, this
is my place, this

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.090
is where software
engineers have to come.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.350
It's kind of like the--

00:04:10.402 --> 00:04:11.360
MARK MANDEL: The mecca?

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.100
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
The mecca, yeah.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.890
So I decided to come here, so
I started looking for a job,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.810
and I found Pocket Gems.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:19.410
They interviewed me, they
hired me, they gave me a visa.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.440
So I moved, and
I've been working

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.160
there for the last
3 and 1/2 years

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.780
making games, and having
fun, and creating stuff.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.370
It's awesome, I love it.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.740
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.980
So I know that you've
been behind a game

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.351
called War Dragons, which has
had some pretty decent success.

00:04:39.351 --> 00:04:40.600
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yes, we did.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:41.280
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.080
Do you want to tell
us a little bit

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.470
about the game, what it's about,
what's the style of the game?

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:45.240
All that sort of fun stuff.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.656
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
OK, so the game,

00:04:46.656 --> 00:04:50.260
War Dragons is a highly
polished, 3D real time strategy

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.150
mobile game where you collect
and control hundreds of dragons

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:54.600
to take out enemy bases.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.820
In short, you get to control a
lot of awesome looking dragons

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.790
and blow up a lot of stuff.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.670
It was Pocket
Gem's first 3D game

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.600
with a real time synchronous
multiplayer battles,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.850
and our first game to come
out with social features

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:07.590
from day one.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.960
Features like chat, guilds,
forums, leaderboards,

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:11.600
all of that.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.170
That's the first game that
on day one we had that.

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.640
Development-wise it was
our longest game from start

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.000
to release.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.260
We never had a game that
had that size before.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.700
We never had a game that
had the size of the team

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.310
that we have right now.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:30.565
So it was a great
learning experience for us

00:05:30.565 --> 00:05:31.880
at Pocket Gems.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:35.640
We had to create
a new 3D engine,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.500
we had to create a lot of
real time communication stuff

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.560
or for mobile.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.284
It was a really exciting
project to work on.

00:05:42.284 --> 00:05:43.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.400
So I wanted to add also
since you just showed us

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.700
that on top of having
so many cool features,

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.330
you also have a very cool ad,
and I thought it was amazing.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.290
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
Thank you, yeah.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.206
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I would
put that on the show.

00:05:56.206 --> 00:05:58.882
It is super cute and
creepy at the same time.

00:05:58.882 --> 00:05:59.840
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.480
We're trying to reach
an audience of like,

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.860
this is supposed to show on
like Comedy Central, and ESPN.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.480
So you imagine you are like
a person, like 30 years old,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:14.550
and you're watching ESPN, and
that thing comes on, it's like,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.400
it's kind of weird,
you have to watch it.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.420
You understand what I'm saying?

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.860
But it's probably going to get
your attention a little bit.

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:20.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.570
We'll put it on the show
notes, that's for sure.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:23.450
MARK MANDEL: It's
definitely worth watching.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:23.970
It's hilarious.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:25.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Really made me laugh.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.850
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: We
like it, we like it.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:27.766
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:27.766 --> 00:06:30.150
So you said it's
an Android game,

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.460
and I'm interested on the
Android side of it too.

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.430
But could you
explain a little bit

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:38.010
about since it's an
Android game, what

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:39.890
is the part on the Cloud?

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:43.186
Like, why are you using
the Cloud, and how?

00:06:43.186 --> 00:06:45.060
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: So
it's on Android and iOS.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.660
We started with iOS, we just
released our Android version

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.690
roughly a month ago.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.220
And the Cloud
basically allows us

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:56.840
to-- we need the Cloud to
do all the social features.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:01.840
We need to cloud, because it's
a game where everybody's bases

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.310
matter, and everything.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:07.300
Like you can affect the
game of other players.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.230
It's a very connected game.

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:10.890
We need to have
that connectivity.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:14.120
We need to have all the Cloud
to support those features.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.930
So it's very, very heavily
based on the Cloud.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:21.460
Like we have a lot of features
that are based on the Cloud.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.110
We like to release new events.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:26.560
For example, every week
we have special events,

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.460
and all of our special
events are heavily

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:30.250
based on Cloud technology.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.030
They're not built on the phone.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:34.270
We have something very
generic on the phone,

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:38.650
and then we can just push
different events every week

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.670
just using our cloud technology.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:46.060
It also allows us to have
Android and iOS players playing

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.270
together at the same time.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.220
So it doesn't matter if
you're playing Android

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.980
or if you're playing iOS,
you're going to battle people

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.340
within other platforms, as well.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:56.090
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.260
And it also allows you to do
a lot of stuff with analytics

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.230
as well, doesn't it?

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:00.188
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:02.020
We've been doing
analytics on the Cloud

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:03.478
since the beginning
of Pocket Gems.

00:08:03.478 --> 00:08:05.260
Like basically, we
always have a lot

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:09.240
of heavy analytics on the
Cloud for all of our games.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.280
So we have already this
built into all of our games.

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.770
We have a common platform
that every game sends

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:16.440
all their events,
and all the data

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.120
that we need to figure out
what are users enjoying here,

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:22.680
what are users not enjoying?

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.320
We use it to do AB testing.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.450
To see if a new feature is
something that the users are

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.530
going to like or not.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:33.034
And then we decide to release
features based on that data.

00:08:33.034 --> 00:08:33.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:37.490
Are you also using analytics
not only for features, but also

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.341
to analyze performance,
and stuff like that?

00:08:40.341 --> 00:08:41.299
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:43.150
So one part of the
analytics that we do

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:45.840
is collect performance
from devices.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.880
So we can split by device
type, and by platform,

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.940
and we know what
places of the game

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.930
are slowing down
the user, or places

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:59.100
that are using too much memory.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:02.500
So we can also use that data
to drive our development

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:06.210
to make our game perform
faster, have better FPS.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:10.020
We're always trying to have
very smooth animation, which

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:13.044
is really hard with the range
of devices that we work with.

00:09:13.044 --> 00:09:14.710
Like, if you have a
very high end device

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:16.200
it's easy to do that.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.810
But when you go to
the lower end devices

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.470
that we still want to support
because still a lot of people

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.450
are using those devices, it
becomes really, really hard.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:28.770
And having all that data to help
us focus on an area of the game

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.660
that we need to improve
performance and make

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:33.709
the game smoother for everyone.

00:09:33.709 --> 00:09:34.500
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.970
So obviously you've got a
back end infrastructure.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.150
You're on our podcast,
which probably means you're

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:40.590
using Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:40.993
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yes

00:09:40.993 --> 00:09:43.440
MARK MANDEL: Do you want to
tell us a little bit about what

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.815
backend infrastructure you're
using, how you're using it,

00:09:45.815 --> 00:09:46.620
that sort of stuff?

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.060
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah, we've
been using Google App Engine

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.280
heavily since the
beginning of Pocket Gems.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.100
So from the first game that
when the company was just

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.050
two people, the two
founders, they started

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.620
using Google App Engine.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.360
And we haven't looked back.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.740
Like, Google App Engine
has worked for us really,

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:06.510
really well, and has continued
to work with every game

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:07.630
that we build.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:13.230
So use Google App Engine a
lot, we use Datastore a lot.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:16.620
We use probably every feature
that Google App Engine has

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.650
to offer, we are using
in very different ways,

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.490
very, we think,
innovative ways that we've

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.952
used Google App Engine.

00:10:26.952 --> 00:10:28.410
I don't know if
you want to mention

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.920
the talk we did at GCP Next.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:30.920
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:31.330
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:32.510
So there's that talk.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:33.970
We talk a lot about that.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:37.140
We go into some more
technical details,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.270
we have some interesting tricks
that we use at Pocket Gems

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:44.030
to be able to run our games
in a smooth way in the Cloud.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:47.008
So I think we're also going
to put that on the show notes.

00:10:47.008 --> 00:10:48.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.322
So something I'm curious
about, and I always

00:10:50.322 --> 00:10:51.780
ask this question
every single time

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.070
I see people doing real time.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:55.844
How are you doing
it on App Engine?

00:10:55.844 --> 00:10:57.760
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: So the
part about real time,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.540
we're using a company
called PubNub.

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:02.180
So PubNub does a lot of
real time stuff as well.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.430
So because we already had
a partnership with them,

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:07.310
we decided to use
that for real time.

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:11.550
And I don't know what they have
in their backend, to be honest.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.670
But we do some stuff which is
near real time in Google App

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:16.690
Engine.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.750
It has low latency, so you
can do stuff like that.

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:21.430
If you're using it
in a clever way,

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:25.080
like we do a lot of stuff that
requires us to be real time,

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.030
like all the battle
reports and know whenever

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.910
you're fighting someone you
need to distribute resources.

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:34.270
That's all done using
the Google App Engine.

00:11:34.270 --> 00:11:37.690
MARK MANDEL: So you're
essentially doing all your user

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:40.559
data, and your game data,
and your matchmaking,

00:11:40.559 --> 00:11:41.850
all that stuff's in App Engine?

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:42.808
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:11:42.808 --> 00:11:45.200
All that part is in App Engine.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.970
Matchmaking, whenever you
need to battle someone,

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:51.860
to figure out who is a good
user for you to battle.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:54.380
Because we have millions
of users, and some of them

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.650
have super strong
bases, and some of them

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.864
have super you know weak
bases, because they just

00:11:58.864 --> 00:11:59.530
started playing.

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:01.250
So you need to figure that out.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.010
That is all done using
Google App Engine.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.700
We have a leader boards, which
is also very computationally

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.190
heavy.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.350
We use that on Google
App Engine, actually

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:13.290
using some code that Google
released as open source

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:14.450
for leaderboards.

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.406
So we found that when
we were building our

00:12:16.406 --> 00:12:18.780
leaderboard [? tac, ?] and we
said, yeah, let's use that.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.110
So we started using that, and
we modified that a little bit

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:22.920
to suit our needs.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:26.320
But that's the base
of our leaderboard.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.350
What else is the very
complicated there?

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:29.810
Well, all the game user data.

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:33.490
We do support people playing
in multiple devices, that's

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.020
also done through the Cloud.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.240
So whenever you quit your
game, we save all your data

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:38.877
in the Cloud.

00:12:38.877 --> 00:12:40.710
And if you load that
game in another device,

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.860
we load all that
data from the Cloud

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:46.210
so all that game
state management

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.360
is done on Google App Engine.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.560
All our AB testing is done
using Google App Engine.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.470
We can release new
dragons, for example,

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:58.150
without releasing a new
version of the game.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.440
We can do something like that.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.090
And it's all done using
Google App Engine.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:06.410
Even our asset management is
done using Google App Engine,

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:08.300
and using the Google
frontend cache.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:09.504
It's really fast for us.

00:13:09.504 --> 00:13:10.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:12.310
So I'm assuming that
when you say Google App

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:17.140
Engine to store data, you're
actually using the Datastore?

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:18.640
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
For the files, I'm

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.247
not sure how we're doing that.

00:13:20.247 --> 00:13:21.830
I'm not sure if it's
Google Datastore.

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.201
I think it is actually
using Google Datastore.

00:13:24.201 --> 00:13:25.950
MARK MANDEL: Could be
Cloud Storage maybe.

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:26.420
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:27.150
I'm not sure.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.170
That part I don't know.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:31.279
That part is really-- we
built that in the beginning.

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:32.570
I never had to touch that code.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:34.920
This has been working for us
for like six years, I think.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:35.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It just works.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.961
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah, it's
just been working forever.

00:13:37.961 --> 00:13:39.910
MARK MANDEL: That is good.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:43.660
So obviously you've
had some success.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:47.031
How have you had to change how
your application has been built

00:13:47.031 --> 00:13:49.530
since it's been running on App
Engine, running on Datastore.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:51.440
Have you needed to make
many changes to it,

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.060
as the scale of how many
loads, and how many players,

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.750
and how many games and
battles have grown?

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:57.830
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: No.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:00.650
At least since I started
working with Pocket Gems,

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:02.600
this has been
working wonderfully.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:04.920
We never had to worry
about our servers

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.110
going down and all of that.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:07.910
Things just work.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:12.827
When we released War Dragons,
we had like big spikes that it's

00:14:12.827 --> 00:14:15.410
shown in that presentation, you
can see the big spikes that we

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:18.290
had in number of
battles, I think,

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.060
is the graph that I had there.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:21.980
And we didn't have
to touch anything.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.140
We didn't have to
call Google and say,

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:26.410
hey, we're going to
have a big spike here,

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:28.190
so you guys better be ready.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:32.700
It works, and it scales up and
scales down as you need it,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.160
and things just work.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:38.880
We had a few adjustments
that we did basically

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.240
to control the costs that we
have in Google App Engine.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.470
So there is some stuff
that we were doing,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:50.395
and the way that we designed
it, it was using too much data,

00:14:50.395 --> 00:14:54.010
or it was doing too
many Datastore calls.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:58.380
So we redesigned the few of
those things to do less calls

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.480
and still have the
same functionality

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:01.630
to drive our costs down.

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:02.910
MARK MANDEL: I believe
you're using memcache a lot

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:03.470
to do that, right?

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:04.830
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: We
use memcache to do that.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.079
It's also in the presentation.

00:15:06.079 --> 00:15:08.560
If people want some
detail on how we do that,

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:12.240
it's a very clever way to
use Memcache, and Datastore,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.380
and Task Use.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:15.600
So we use all that
technology, and we

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.579
were able to drive
our cost down a lot.

00:15:17.579 --> 00:15:18.370
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:22.080
I'm also really curious, I
mean, you just released Android,

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:25.050
you've had an iOS version
out for a long time.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:27.140
Did you have to
change the App Engine

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:30.660
code at all for each
individual platform,

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:31.720
for your mobile platform?

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:32.570
Or was it the same?

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:33.570
Or what happened there?

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:34.778
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Not at all.

00:15:34.778 --> 00:15:36.506
It doesn't change anything.

00:15:36.506 --> 00:15:38.660
You can imagine we have
a bunch of client code

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:41.410
that we say, if is
Android, do this.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:43.670
If it's iOS, do this.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:47.580
But on our platform side, on
the Google App Engine side,

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.820
I don't believe we
have any of that.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.364
All the communication is
done completely the same.

00:15:52.364 --> 00:15:53.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:55.488
Could you tell us it a bit
about how you're doing it?

00:15:55.488 --> 00:15:57.390
Is it a REST API?

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.120
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
It's mostly REST API.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.840
It's really easy to build
REST APIs on top of Google App

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:07.600
Engine, so we mostly use RESTful
APIs with a few exceptions

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.990
there, for example, just
to get files is not REST,

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.300
it's just plain old HTTP.

00:16:14.300 --> 00:16:17.080
There's not a lot of
stuff going on there.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.860
But for all the
data communication,

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:26.140
it's all doing REST, and
with JSON as the format

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:27.950
that we send back and forth.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:31.360
And it's really easy to use
on the mobile side as well.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:37.150
There's millions and thousands
of libraries out there

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:39.860
that's going to do that for
you right out of the box

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:43.077
without using anything crazy.

00:16:43.077 --> 00:16:45.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
question that I always ask, too,

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:48.670
for people that write
code on App Engine,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.400
are you writing it in Go?

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:52.494
Is it written in Go?

00:16:52.494 --> 00:16:53.910
What is the language
that you use?

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:54.860
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: We use Python.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:55.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Python, cool.

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:56.580
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO:
Yeah, we use Python.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:57.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's fine, it's fine.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.166
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: No,
Python is awesome.

00:16:59.166 --> 00:16:59.800
I love Python.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:01.174
I haven't written
anything in Go,

00:17:01.174 --> 00:17:04.890
so I'm not going to badmouth it.

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Excellent.

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:08.810
So you do some really
interesting things.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.035
We covered this in
our presentation,

00:17:11.035 --> 00:17:12.410
but I'd like to
go over it again,

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.160
because I think it
was really clever.

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.670
How you use App Engine
versions, and match those up

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:17.650
to client versions.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.900
Do you want to talk a little
bit about how that works?

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:20.858
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Sure.

00:17:20.858 --> 00:17:24.819
So Google App Engine versions,
for people that don't know,

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:29.620
it's basically, you can deploy
various versions of your code

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.190
in Google App Engine,
then Google App Engine's

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:34.710
going to give you a different
URL for every version

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:35.680
that you have.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:37.630
Traditionally, this
is used for testing.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.290
So if I'm a developer and
I'm developing a new feature,

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:42.780
I don't want to keep
deploying that to production.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:47.660
So we deploy that to a different
version of Google App Engine,

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:50.390
and then I can test that
using that different URL,

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:53.130
without disturbing
production code.

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:57.250
But at Pocket Gems, we devised
a different use for that.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:18:01.910
So whenever we release a new
version on mobile, what happens

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:04.030
is sometimes we have
to break compatibility

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:07.290
with the previous version
of Google App Engine.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:10.340
And traditionally, people have
been doing that with if else,

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:12.294
if else, if version
is this, do this.

00:18:12.294 --> 00:18:13.460
If version is that, do that.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.251
And then you end up
with a lot of code that

00:18:15.251 --> 00:18:17.920
is old code, and awful to read.

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:19.180
So we don't like that.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:20.880
So what we do is, we
basically, whenever

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:23.260
we release a new version
of Google App Engine,

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:25.690
we don't care about
breaking compatibility.

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:27.560
And what we do is
when we release

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.270
a new version of
the game, we first

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:35.890
deploy the new version we
deploy in a different URL,

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:41.290
and we configure our client
code to go to that URL instead.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.060
So then when people start
downloading that new version,

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.480
they go to the new version
of Google App Engine.

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:48.910
And the old version still
go to the old version,

00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:51.840
so we don't break
compatibility there.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.670
It all works for everyone.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.240
And then at some
point, what we do

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.190
is we force users to upgrade.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.750
So a few days after
we release something,

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:01.480
we force users to upgrade.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:03.050
Then everybody
upgrades, and we were

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.200
able to take down
the old version.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.140
And now the new
version becomes what

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.410
is called the default
version, and everybody just

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.150
connects to that version.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.960
So we're able to very
easily break compatibility

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:17.942
without actually
breaking anything.

00:19:17.942 --> 00:19:19.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.900
I guess that that's the power
of being able to force clients

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:24.497
to be updated.

00:19:24.497 --> 00:19:26.580
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah,
we didn't have to do that.

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:29.940
We only do that because
also we sometimes break

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.410
compatibility in battle.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:36.530
So when you're battling people
online with the new version

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:39.020
might have a different
spell that we created.

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:42.290
And then if I'm fighting you
when you have the old version,

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:44.600
and I do that
spell, that spell's

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:46.340
not going to work on your end.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:48.070
So now things
become out of sync.

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:50.380
So we break
compatibility basically

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:51.859
not because of server code.

00:19:51.859 --> 00:19:53.400
Like, if you think
about server code,

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:58.220
we could just keep 10
versions running there.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:02.910
It wouldn't cost us much, or
anything else, I think, more,

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:04.900
and it wouldn't be
such a big deal.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.650
But there's this
client part that

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:09.840
does break compatibility
when people

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.530
are playing with each other
online that would break.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:14.699
So that's why we force
people to upgrade, basically.

00:20:14.699 --> 00:20:16.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is interesting.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:19.060
Otherwise, people could
use the strategy of,

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:21.580
I'm not going to upgrade,
so no one can attack me.

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:22.538
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:20:22.538 --> 00:20:25.940
But then you can't
attack anyone, and yeah.

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:27.689
You lose a lot if
you don't do that.

00:20:27.689 --> 00:20:29.480
And the social aspect
of the game, I think,

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.380
it's one of the most
important parts.

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.610
We've been seeing that
that social aspect of it

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.510
is what drives people back.

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:40.280
Like we have guilds, too, so
everybody is part of a guild,

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:42.640
and your guild
just plays together

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:45.650
to fight all other guilds,
and your guild members

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:48.980
can help you, and you
attack with their dragons.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:52.010
So that aspect is really
important for everyone.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:54.850
So you can see people, whenever
we release a new version,

00:20:54.850 --> 00:20:56.740
I get a message from
my guild leader saying,

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:58.156
hey, you should
upgrade your game,

00:20:58.156 --> 00:21:00.430
because otherwise
we can't help you.

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:03.100
So upgrade right now so we
can all still help each other.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:05.560
So that aspect is really nice.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.960
So upgrading is important
for users, not just for us.

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:09.960
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:14.580
So we also covered this
in the presentation,

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:17.530
but again, I thought this
was really interesting.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:21.250
You used Datastore in some
really interesting ways

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:22.575
to do matchmaking.

00:21:22.575 --> 00:21:24.700
Go on and talk a little
bit how that process works.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:26.790
I know you also use task queues
and some other fun stuff.

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:27.080
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:29.910
So for matchmaking, it's
kind of a complicated thing

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.230
because what I said before.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.330
You have millions of
users, and every user's

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.640
going to have a different level.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.060
So what we created,
basically we created

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:44.190
a concept of bucketing users
into similar matchmaking

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:45.070
levels.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.620
So whenever you
do something that

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.570
changes what your power
would be, like for example,

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:53.560
you upgraded a building,
or you upgraded a dragon,

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:58.650
or you just applied
some different

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:00.810
what we call runes to
a dragon, for example,

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:02.530
your powers change there.

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:04.100
You become more powerful.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:05.870
And whenever something
like that happens,

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:09.750
we put a task in the task
queue to basically recalculate

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.470
what bucket you should be in.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:13.700
And then at some point,
that task queue runs,

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:15.560
we will recalculate
your power, and we

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:19.170
see in what bucket is
better for you to be.

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:23.440
And then we place
you in that bucket.

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.130
That bucket now is
something that whenever

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:29.010
you want to battle someone,
we just go to that bucket

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:30.970
and we pick some player
that's in that bucket.

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:35.300
We know that they're going
to have a very similar level

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.342
to you, so the battle is
going to be challenging.

00:22:37.342 --> 00:22:38.883
It's not going to
be something that's

00:22:38.883 --> 00:22:40.590
going to be super
easy for you to beat,

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:42.548
or it's going to be
impossible for you to beat.

00:22:42.548 --> 00:22:44.814
We know that it's
possible to beat,

00:22:44.814 --> 00:22:46.980
you're going to lose some,
you're going to win some.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:48.630
But it's possible.

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:52.250
But with millions of users,
we couldn't find a better way

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:56.612
to do that and
still perform well.

00:22:56.612 --> 00:22:58.070
Because whenever
you battle, that's

00:22:58.070 --> 00:22:59.444
one of the most
important things.

00:22:59.444 --> 00:23:00.750
We need to respond really fast.

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:04.220
We want you to go into
battle as fast as we can.

00:23:04.220 --> 00:23:08.510
So if we have to
wait for the server

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:11.200
to come back with something,
and that takes five seconds,

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:11.700
it's gone.

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:14.419
Like your user's already out
of the game by that point.

00:23:14.419 --> 00:23:15.210
MARK MANDEL: Great.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.809
So you're able to
leverage task used

00:23:16.809 --> 00:23:18.600
to be able to push that
into the background

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:20.500
so that the user doesn't
have to wait for it,

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.584
and we can keep that
complicated data processing

00:23:22.584 --> 00:23:23.750
happening in the background.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:24.060
GUSTAVO AMBROZIO: Yeah.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:27.010
And it's not that important you
change your bucket right then,

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:27.510
right?

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:29.090
You can wait a few seconds.

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:32.190
Your previous bucket
is still competitive,

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:34.630
it's still very similar
to what you are.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:36.060
You're not going
to be super easy.

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:40.120
So if it takes five, 10
seconds to change your bucket,

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:41.442
it's not a big deal.

00:23:41.442 --> 00:23:43.150
So we just pushed that
to the task queue,

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:45.108
and the task queue does
that calculation for us

00:23:45.108 --> 00:23:46.588
in the background.

00:23:46.588 --> 00:23:47.920
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

