00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:36.441
was immediately curious
about web frameworks in Go.

00:25:36.441 --> 00:25:38.440
It's a common question,
I've heard it definitely

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:39.809
a few times before.

00:25:39.809 --> 00:25:42.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it's
actually a very interesting

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:44.830
question, because especially
for people coming from Python,

00:25:44.830 --> 00:25:49.290
and even more coming
from Ruby, they

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:51.990
tend to miss these frameworks.

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:55.090
Such as you're coming from Ruby,
you know there's Ruby on Rails.

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:58.120
If you come in from
Python, there's Django.

00:25:58.120 --> 00:26:01.640
If you're writing Go, what's up?

00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:08.092
So actually my advice on
this is the first thing

00:26:08.092 --> 00:26:10.300
to understand is actually
the difference between what

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:12.350
a framework and a toolkit is.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:14.400
And while in many
languages you're

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:18.170
going to be finding frameworks--
such as Ruby on Rails

00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:20.280
is clearly a
framework-- frameworks

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:23.560
will force you to adapt
your code to whatever they

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:26.220
feel the code should look like.

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:28.600
So that is something
that if that

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:30.870
is what you're trying to
follow, that totally fine.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.170
And if your use case matches
what they're providing,

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:34.250
then it's great.

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:37.520
Now, when you try to modify
it, might be really hard.

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.900
So instead, what the
Go community tries

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:43.370
to drive towards is toolkits.

00:26:43.370 --> 00:26:45.190
So you have a bunch of toolkits.

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:47.527
You have things like my
favorite one is Gorilla.

00:26:47.527 --> 00:26:48.610
MARK MANDEL: Love gorilla.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:49.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:49.250 --> 00:26:50.230
It's a web framework.

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:52.030
It's very, very simple,
and basically you

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:53.904
can pick and choose
whatever you need to use.

00:26:53.904 --> 00:26:55.550
So you have like,
oh, I need to use

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:58.676
a different way of
routing requests

00:26:58.676 --> 00:26:59.920
toward my web handlers.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.390
There's a way of doing that.

00:27:02.390 --> 00:27:04.590
There's I need to do
sessions, or whatever.

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:06.510
There's a bunch of
different tiny packages

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.420
that basically you
can add to your code,

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:13.130
and they'll integrate very
well with the standard net HTTP

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:13.980
package.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:15.080
So that is great.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.050
And if you want to
know more about others,

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.580
there's actually
a bunch of them.

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:23.620
We will put a link to a post
from last year from The New

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:28.460
Stack that basically, they
cover five Go frameworks.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:31.550
And there's Beagle, which
is an actual framework.

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:35.540
There's a Martini, which is a
very lightweight framework that

00:27:35.540 --> 00:27:37.330
uses a lot of reflection.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:42.680
It's kind of like trying
to be Go on Rails,

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.720
and it did not work that well.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:50.950
So the author of the framework,
Jeremy [? Signs-- ?] hi,

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:54.767
Jeremy-- he created
a second version.

00:27:54.767 --> 00:27:55.600
It's called Negroni.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.860
It's much simpler, it's
much more lightweight.

00:27:57.860 --> 00:28:00.580
So if you think about
using something that's out,

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.010
Negroni's also a
very good option.

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:06.040
There is Go Craft, and
then there is also Gorilla.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.310
So check it out,
